Jaya (जय) and Vijaya (विजय) are the celebrated gatekeepers of Vaikuṇṭha, the divine abode of Viṣṇu, in Vaiṣṇava Hindu traditions. They are best known from the story in which they prevent the sages known as the four Kumāras from entering Vaikuṇṭha. The resulting curse leads to their famous births as adversaries of Viṣṇu on Earth.

Their story explores several major themes: divine service, the consequences of pride or mistaken judgment, the mysterious relationship between a devotee and God, and the possibility that even apparent opposition to the divine can become part of a larger sacred narrative.

One distinction matters for your research archive: Jaya and Vijaya belong to Vaiṣṇava theology, not automatically to the Bhairavagaṇa. They are associated with Viṣṇu's divine retinue and the guardianship of Vaikuṇṭha. Their role as gatekeepers makes them comparable to other divine guardians, but does not make them members of Bhairava's retinue.

1. Names and meaning
Jaya (जय): victory, triumph, or conquest.

Vijaya (विजय): victory, success, or overcoming an obstacle.

Together, their names can be interpreted as “Victory and Triumph.” They are known as the dvārapālas (gatekeepers) and pārṣadas (attendants or associates) of Viṣṇu.

2. The story of their curse
The principal account appears in the Śrīmad-Bhāgavatam, Canto 3, Chapters 15–16. 

